/* Carries run.sh's expected-refusal marker text and MUST STILL COMPILE.

   The marker is what makes pwr12 and pwr24 readable as measurements
   rather than as breakage. But those two designs fail for their own
   reasons, so neither can show that the marker itself is inert to CUPL
   rather than a syntax error being mistaken for the refusal under test.
   A control that must succeed can.

   This is in2 with the marker added. Expected: a JEDEC identical to
   in2's, with the literal intact at fuse 92 (pin 2, row 2, column 4).

   The marker text is spelled out below in a form run.sh does NOT match,
   so this file is not itself treated as an expected refusal -- run.sh
   requires the words alone on their own line.

   Marker under test:  EXPECT refusal (inline, so it does not match) */

PIN 2 = i0;
PIN 23 = o0;

o0 = i0;
